75	// The complete set of top-level settings .claudinite-checks.json may carry. A key
76	// outside this set is a typo or a stale name — a settings error as real as invalid
77	// JSON, caught at load so it can't silently change nothing. `packConfig` is the
78	// legacy home of per-pack parameters — still honored while the fleet migrates to
79	// pack-entry `config` (the baselining folds it in), no longer documented. The
80	// `pack-entry-config` baseline migration (migrations/active_migrations/) tracks
81	// the fleet's convergence; when it retires, drop the key here (and the overlay
82	// below) so a straggler gets the unknown-setting error.
83	// `claudinite` is the vendored-mount stamp — { updated: "<ISO datetime>", ref: "<sha>" },
84	// written by the nightly update pass, selecting which migration notes still apply
85	// (vendoring/DESIGN.md owns the model); the checks engine itself only tolerates it.
86	// `taskScheduler` is the per-repo maintenance anchor the vendored hourly scheduler
87	// reads (per-project-scheduling DESIGN §2) — { dailyHour, weeklyDay, monthlyDay },
88	// all UTC. Absence means the documented defaults, so an omitted key is not an
89	// error; a present one is range-validated at load below. Its declaration is also
90	// the per-repo cutover marker during the scheduling rollout (MIGRATION Phase 0.6).
91	// A repo's README pack-badge row has no key here on purpose: the row is seeded at
92	// adoption and owned by the repo after, so there is nothing for it to configure.
93	// A member carrying a stale `badges` key therefore gets the unknown-setting error
94	// below, and the wiring converge (engine/scheduler/converge-wiring.mjs) clears it.
95	// `dormant` is the project's own declaration that it is out of the RECURRING work —
96	// see isDormant below for exactly how much that covers.
97	export const CONFIG_KEYS = ['packs', 'rules', 'accept', 'sharedConstants', 'packConfig', 'maintenance', 'claudinite', 'taskScheduler', 'dormant'];
98	
99	// Does this project declare itself DORMANT? A project goes dormant when it is
100	// finished, parked, or simply not being worked on: it should stop paying the
101	// upkeep of a project that IS being worked on.
102	//
103	// What dormancy means, exactly — it is narrow on purpose:
104	//   - NO RECURRING WORK. The vendored scheduler stops before it evaluates
105	//     anything (engine/scheduler/run.mjs), so no task is due, no dispatch issue
106	//     is filed, no agent session is started, and no maintenance PR is opened.
107	//     Nothing scheduled runs "for nothing" on a repo nobody is working on.
108	//   - NO FLEET CEREMONY. Whatever looks at this repo from the OUTSIDE reads the
109	//     same declaration and leaves it alone rather than reporting it as unhealthy —
110	//     a repo TOLD to stop keeping up must not then be nagged for not keeping up.
111	//     Which is why this predicate is exported for a raw declaration too (below).
112	//   - EVERYTHING ELSE STAYS ON. Claudinite is not switched off: the session
113	//     hooks, the checks engine, the mounted skills and pack prose all work
114	//     exactly as before the moment someone opens a session on the repo. Dormancy
115	//     is about unattended upkeep, never about what an interactive session may do.
116	//
117	// The predicate reads BOTH shapes deliberately: a raw parsed .claudinite-checks.json
118	// and the normalized config loadConfig returns. A cross-repo reader fetches another
119	// repo's declaration over the API with no engine loaded against that tree, and it
120	// must decide dormancy by the same test that repo's own scheduler used — a second
121	// notion of dormancy would nag exactly the repos that had already opted out. One
122	// definition, both sides.
123	export const isDormant = (config) => config?.dormant === true;
124	
125	// The keys a `schedule` object may carry, and the canonical weekday vocabulary
126	// (mirrored from engine/scheduler/slots.mjs WEEKDAYS — kept as a literal here so
127	// the checks layer does not import the scheduler engine).
